---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CopyCode from '../../components/CopyCode.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const code = 'text-sm bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded';
const link = 'text-vibrant-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-vibrant-800';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="CAIL Pi - CUNY AI Lab"
  description="CAIL Pi adds CUNY AI Lab models, web search, and questions from Pi to Pi, a terminal coding agent."
>
  <section class="relative pt-24 pb-10 lg:pt-28 lg:pb-12">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-br from-cuny-blue via-cuny-blue to-vibrant-800"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-[0.04]" aria-hidden="true" style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=&quot;40&quot; height=&quot;40&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 40 40&quot; xmlns=&quot;http://www.w3.org/2000/svg&quot;%3E%3Cpath d=&quot;M20 20.5V18H0v-2h20v-2.5L25 18l-5 2.5z&quot; fill=&quot;%23ffffff&quot; fill-opacity=&quot;1&quot;/%3E%3C/svg%3E');"></div>
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 relative z-10">
      <a href={`${base}resources/`} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-vibrant-300 hover:text-white text-sm font-medium mb-4 transition-colors">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 19l-7-7m0 0l7-7m-7 7h18" />
        </svg>
        Back to Resources
      </a>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold font-display text-white tracking-tight">CAIL Pi</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-vibrant-200 text-lg max-w-2xl">Pi is an open-source coding agent that runs in your terminal. CAIL Pi is a Pi package that adds the Lab's models, web search, and a way for Pi to ask you questions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-neutral-cream">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10 lg:py-14">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_15rem] gap-8 items-start">
        <article class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
          <div class="p-6 md:p-9 space-y-10">
            <section aria-labelledby="what-heading">
              <h2 id="what-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">What it is</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Pi is similar to Claude Code. It is open source and can use models from many providers. With CAIL Pi installed, it gets:</p>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
                <li>CUNY AI Lab models through the Lab's gateway. Pi asks the gateway for the current list each time it starts, so new models appear without an update.</li>
                <li>Web search and page reading. Searches go through Exa's free service and need no key of their own.</li>
                <li>A way for Pi to ask you to choose between options before it goes ahead.</li>
                <li>The <code class={code}>/cail</code> command, which checks your setup.</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">The code is on <a href="https://github.com/CUNY-AI-Lab/cail-pi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>GitHub</a> and the package is on <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>npm</a>.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="prereq-heading">
              <h2 id="prereq-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Before you start</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">You need a CUNY AI Lab account with model access. If you don't have one yet, <a href={`${base}request-access/`} class={link}>request access</a>. Pi's installer takes care of everything else, including Node.js when your computer needs it.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="install-heading">
              <h2 id="install-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Install</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Open a terminal (Terminal on a Mac, PowerShell on Windows) and install Pi with its own installer. Press Enter to accept the installer's suggestions.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Install Pi on macOS or Linux">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>curl -fsSL https://pi.dev/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <div class="h-4"></div>
              <CopyCode label="Install Pi on Windows PowerShell">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>irm https://pi.dev/install.ps1 | iex</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4 mb-4">Then add the CAIL Pi package. The same command works on every system.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Add CAIL Pi">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi install npm:@cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">If the terminal says it can't find <code class={code}>pi</code>, close the window, open a new one, and run the command again.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="signin-heading">
              <h2 id="signin-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Sign in</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Go to the folder you want to work in and start Pi.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Start Pi">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">
                <li>Type <code class={code}>/login cail</code> and choose <strong>Sign in with CUNY AI Lab</strong>.</li>
                <li>Your browser opens the Lab's sign-in page. Sign in with your CUNY Login.</li>
                <li>Click <strong>Connect Pi</strong>, then go back to Pi.</li>
              </ol>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Pi says it logged in "but no default model is configured for provider "cail"". You can ignore that message. Type your first message, and Pi answers with DeepSeek V4 Flash.</p>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Signing in gives Pi its own personal key, named "Pi on" followed by your computer's name. It appears in <a href="https://tools.ailab.gc.cuny.edu/model-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>your dashboard</a> with your other keys and expires after 180 days. When it expires, Pi asks you to sign in again.</p>
              <dl class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">Working on another computer over SSH</dt><dd>Pi shows a link and asks for a code. Open the link in a browser on any computer, click <strong>Connect Pi</strong>, and paste the code the page shows into Pi.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">Using an API key you already have</dt><dd>Choose <strong>Sign in with an API key</strong> in <code class={code}>/login cail</code> and paste a personal key from your dashboard. The <a href={`${base}docs/api-keys`} class={link}>API key guide</a> explains how to create one.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="model-heading">
              <h2 id="model-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Choose a model</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Type <code class={code}>/model</code> to switch to another CUNY AI Lab model. Pi starts each session on the newest DeepSeek Flash unless you've saved a default model, which you do by pressing <code class={code}>Ctrl+S</code> in <code class={code}>/model</code>. Every request counts against your monthly allowance, and your dashboard shows how much is left.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="update-heading">
              <h2 id="update-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Updating</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">This updates Pi and all of your Pi packages, CAIL Pi included. Close any window where Pi is running first. Your sign-in, settings, and sessions stay as they are.</p>
              <CopyCode label="Update Pi and its packages">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi update --all</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">To update only CAIL Pi, run <code class={code}>pi update npm:@cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code>.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="earlier-heading">
              <h2 id="earlier-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">If you used the earlier installer</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Earlier versions of CAIL Pi were installed with <code class={code}>npx @cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code>, which also ran LazyPi. That installer is gone. Your Pi and your saved key keep working, and three steps bring the setup up to date.</p>
              <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <li>Run <code class={code}>pi update --all</code>, as above. If Pi says it "cannot self-update this installation", update it with <code class={code}>npm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent@latest</code> (<code class={code}>npm.cmd</code> on Windows), then run <code class={code}>pi update --extensions</code>.</li>
                <li>
                  CAIL Pi now includes web search and the ask-user tool, and LazyPi installed its own copies of both. Remove those copies, or Pi reports "Tool … conflicts" errors each time it starts. If a command says the package isn't installed, use the name <code class={code}>pi list</code> shows:
                  <div class="mt-3">
                  <CopyCode label="Remove the separate copies">
                  <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi remove npm:pi-web-access
pi remove npm:pi-ask-user</code></pre>
                  </CopyCode>
                  </div>
                </li>
                <li>Type <code class={code}>pi list</code> to see your other packages. LazyPi installed several, and some of them print warnings when Pi starts. Remove the ones you don't use with <code class={code}>pi remove</code> and the name <code class={code}>pi list</code> shows. If the list includes <code class={code}>npm:@cuny-ai-lab/pi-workshop</code>, remove it too: it loads the CUNY AI Lab provider a second time.</li>
              </ol>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">To switch from your pasted key to signing in, run <code class={code}>/login cail</code> and choose <strong>Sign in with CUNY AI Lab</strong>. Your old key stays in your dashboard until you revoke it.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="trouble-heading">
              <h2 id="trouble-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Troubleshooting</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Start by typing <code class={code}>/cail</code> inside Pi. It reports whether the gateway answers, whether your key is saved and accepted, how many CUNY AI Lab models you can use, and which model is active. On Windows it also checks Pi's PowerShell tool. It never shows your key.</p>
              <dl class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"No API key found for the selected model"</dt><dd>Run <code class={code}>/login cail</code> and sign in.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"Your CUNY AI Lab sign-in has expired"</dt><dd>The key from signing in lasts 180 days. Run <code class={code}>/login cail</code> and sign in again.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"Your CUNY AI Lab access is not active yet"</dt><dd>Signing in works only for accounts with model access. <a href={`${base}request-access/`} class={link}>Request access</a>, or write to the Lab if you've already been approved.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"That API key was not accepted"</dt><dd>This applies to a pasted key. Look for missing or extra characters and paste it again, or sign in with your CUNY Login instead.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">No CUNY AI Lab models in /model</dt><dd>Pi lists them once you've signed in. If <code class={code}>/cail</code> says your key is valid but the list stays empty, restart Pi.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">"Tool … conflicts" when Pi starts</dt><dd>A separate copy of web search or the ask-user tool is installed. Remove it as described under <a href="#earlier-heading" class={link}>If you used the earlier installer</a>.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">Pi takes over the whole terminal window</dt><dd>Pi 1.0 does this by default. To keep your terminal's normal scrollback, add <code class={code}>"tuiMode": "regular"</code> to Pi's settings file (<code class={code}>~/.pi/agent/settings.json</code>, or <code class={code}>%USERPROFILE%\.pi\agent\settings.json</code> on Windows), or start Pi with <code class={code}>pi --tui-mode regular</code>.</dd></div>
                <div><dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-stone">Windows: "running scripts is disabled on this system"</dt><dd>Type <code class={code}>pi.cmd</code> in place of <code class={code}>pi</code>. You don't need to change the execution policy.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="windows-heading">
              <h2 id="windows-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">On Windows</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">CAIL Pi turns on Pi's PowerShell tool in <code class={code}>%USERPROFILE%\.pi\agent\settings.json</code>, so the model runs PowerShell commands. If you haven't chosen which tools Pi uses, it sets <code class={code}>"defaultTools"</code> to <code class={code}>["-bash", "+powershell"]</code>. If you have, it adds <code class={code}>"+powershell"</code> to your list, unless the list already mentions PowerShell. It keeps a backup of the previous file next to it. Run <code class={code}>/reload</code> to use the tool in the session where it was turned on.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="remove-heading">
              <h2 id="remove-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Removing CAIL Pi</h2>
              <CopyCode label="Remove the package">
              <pre tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code>pi remove npm:@cuny-ai-lab/cail-pi</code></pre>
              </CopyCode>
              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Your key stays in Pi until you type <code class={code}>/logout</code>. Logging out doesn't revoke the key, so revoke it in your dashboard if you no longer need it.</p>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
              <h2 id="privacy-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-neutral-stone mb-3">Privacy and security</h2>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                <li>Pi saves your key in <code class={code}>~/.pi/agent/auth.json</code>, which only your account can read on macOS and Linux.</li>
                <li>The CUNY AI Lab extension sends your key only to the Lab's gateway: with each model request, and when you run <code class={code}>/cail</code>.</li>
                <li>Signing in again from the same computer replaces that computer's key. A key you pasted yourself is never replaced.</li>
                <li>Paste a sign-in code only into Pi on your own computer, and never send one to anyone.</li>
                <li>Web searches go to Exa. Pages you ask Pi to read are fetched directly from your computer.</li>
                <li>If you prefer, set the <code class={code}>AILAB_API_KEY</code> environment variable. Pi uses it when it has no saved CUNY AI Lab sign-in or key.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </div>
        </article>

        <aside class="lg:sticky lg:top-24 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm p-5" aria-label="Guide sections">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-neutral-stone mb-4">On this page</h2>
          <nav>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
              <li><a href="#what-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">What it is</a></li>
              <li><a href="#prereq-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Before you start</a></li>
              <li><a href="#install-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Install</a></li>
              <li><a href="#signin-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Sign in</a></li>
              <li><a href="#model-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Choose a model</a></li>
              <li><a href="#update-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Updating</a></li>
              <li><a href="#earlier-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">If you used the earlier installer</a></li>
              <li><a href="#trouble-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Troubleshooting</a></li>
              <li><a href="#windows-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">On Windows</a></li>
              <li><a href="#remove-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Removing CAIL Pi</a></li>
              <li><a href="#privacy-heading" class="text-gray-600 hover:text-vibrant-700">Privacy and security</a></li>
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
